Craft/framing rules for Nick's cover letters, banked 2026-06-30 so the same feedback isn't given each round. Full set lives in the career-pivot project file ("Cover-letter craft rules").

- **Match the role's nature; don't impose a lens.** Read what the role rewards first. A delivery/execution role where others own scoping (e.g. a Digital PM at an agency where the MD/BA scope) leads on reliable execution, not gap-closing or strategy. A hands-on SWE role frames domain knowledge as what sharpens the *engineering*, not as product input. Never frame a SWE role as product, or vice versa.
- **Narrative altitude.** PM/delivery/leadership roles lead higher-level (direction, leadership, outcomes) with engineering *supporting* (second); SWE roles lead with engineering. Same proofs, different order.
- **Describe systems, don't just name them.** The reader won't know "Asset Master" or "LIMSOC" — say what each is.
- **One mention per proof.** Don't reference the same engagement twice under two framings (the LIMSOC national-scaling work is one thing, not two).
- **No colons or semicolons in prose** (em dashes already banned). Full stops and commas only.
- **Personalise the salutation** ("Dear [Company] Hiring Team,"); generic only for undisclosed/recruiter roles.
- **Closes: affirmative and poised, never casual.** Confident bet-on-me energy yes, slang no (avoid "back myself", "no hard feelings", "so be it", "blown away", "one of your best hires"). Prefer "I would welcome the chance to show it in person. A conversation will make clear exactly what I mean," + concrete outcome + "a hire you will be glad you made." Use "show", not "prove".
- **Flow over density.** Focused paragraphs with transitions beat dense walls; no clunky label openers ("The engineering is senior and hands-on"); tighten wordiness/repetition.
- **Resume structure:** AccessBuddy in a bottom "Additional Projects" section, never under Experience (main Hybrid fix); no "Selected Engagements" header; current Hybrid headline is "Technology leader bridging business and technology through product and strategy, with a strong engineering foundation, working across enterprise, consumer, and not-for-profit sectors". Use "not-for-profit".
- **Export QA:** pdftotext-check ligatures, every paragraph ends with a full stop, salutation personalised.

